To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function. This function is used to terminate the program with a specified exit status. Here's how you can use it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    // Your program code here
    printf("Program is running...\n");
    // Add your program logic here

    // Terminate the program with a specified exit status
    exit(0); // Exit with status 0, indicating success
    // Or you can specify a different status code
    // exit(1); // Exit with status 1, indicating an error

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the program will print "Program is running..." and then terminate with an exit status of 0, which typically indicates successful execution. You can replace `exit(0)` with `exit(1)` to indicate an error or any other status code as needed.
